# League of Legends has actually been free-to-play since it launched…

League of Legends has actually been free-to-play since it launched back in 2009! That’s been their business model from the start.

The game itself costs nothing to download and play — you get access to all the core gameplay. They make money through:

|  | ? | Skins (cosmetic changes to how champions look) |
| --- | --- | --- |
|  | ? | Champion unlocks — you start with a rotating selection of free champions each week, and can unlock others permanently by playing and earning in-game currency, or by buying them with real money to speed things up |

So yeah, you can play League completely free forever. You’ll just need to either grind a bit to unlock champions you want, or spend money if you want them all immediately. But the game itself and all its competitive features are totally free.

It’s one of the biggest free-to-play success stories and probably has one of the largest gaming communities in the world. The subreddit and Discord communities are massive if you’re looking to connect with other players.

---

## Is League the type of game that appeals to you, or were you thinking of trying something else?

Ah, you’re a fellow MMORPG veteran! Good news and bad news on your options:

### RuneScape / MapleStory vibes
|  | ? | Old School RuneScape is still thriving and free-to-play (membership is like $12/month for full content) |
| --- | --- | --- |
|  | ? | MapleStory is also still around and free |
|  | ? | Guild Wars 2 has a huge free-to-play version with an active community |
|  | ? | Final Fantasy XIV has a free trial up to level 70 (hundreds of hours of content) |

### Assetto Corsa
Yes, there’s definitely community play! It’s primarily known as a racing sim, and the multiplayer scene is active but works differently than MMOs:

|  | ? | Public and private servers for racing with others |
| --- | --- | --- |
|  | ? | Huge modding community (free tracks, cars, etc.) |
|  | ? | Leagues and communities organize regular races |
|  | ? | Discord servers coordinate events and practice sessions |

The base game goes on sale for like $5–10 pretty regularly. Assetto Corsa Competizione is the newer one focused on GT racing with better graphics, but the original Assetto Corsa has way more mods and variety.

However — you’ll want at least a controller for it. A proper racing wheel setup is amazing but not required to start. Many people play with Xbox/PlayStation controllers.

Given your MMORPG background, you might really enjoy the community/progression elements of racing leagues in AC. Very different vibe from RuneScape but the “join a community and improve together” aspect is definitely there.

What drew you to racing sims versus going back to an MMO?

---

## Good question — but honestly, Xbox exclusives aren’t really where MMOs live. Here’s the reality:

### Xbox-exclusive MMOs
Pretty much none. Most MMOs are on PC, and when they come to console, they usually hit both Xbox and PlayStation.

### Xbox advantages for your interests
|  | ? | Game Pass ($10–15/month) is the real draw — includes: |
| --- | --- | --- |
|  | ? | Elder Scrolls Online (great MMO, very active) |
|  | ? | Fallout 76 (MMO-lite) |
|  | ? | Sea of Thieves (multiplayer adventure, very community-focused) |
|  - | ? | Tons of other games to try cheap |

But here’s the thing — the big MMOs you’d probably enjoy are either:
|  | ? | On both consoles (ESO, FFXIV, Black Desert Online) |
| --- | --- | --- |
|  | ? | PC only (OSRS, Guild Wars 2, WoW, Lost Ark) |

Xbox exclusives you might like based on your taste:
|  | ? | Sea of Thieves (community-driven pirate game, very social) |
| --- | --- | --- |
|  | ? | Grounded (survival with friends) |
|  | ? | Halo Infinite multiplayer (free, community aspect) |

Honest take: If MMOs are your main interest, PC is the better platform. Xbox shines more for Game Pass variety and single-player exclusives (Starfield, Forza, Halo campaigns).

PS5 also doesn’t have many MMO exclusives either. The MMO scene is really dominated by PC.

Would you be open to a budget PC instead, or are you set on console for other reasons (couch gaming, simplicity, etc.)?
